## 7. Discrepancies with §1.4 / §1.5 of the Part A prompt

§1.4 anchors: all reproduced ({{checks.n_checks}} checks, {{checks.n_failed}} failed).

1. **§1.5-1 (clamp).** Holds for exploded runs: loss / (1 − train acc) is within 0.3 of 27.63 in
   {{checks.clamp_ratio.s0.mnist.mlp2.n_at_clamp}} Stage 0 and {{report.clamp.s2_cnn_n_at_clamp}} Stage 2 CNN leaky runs with final train loss > ln 10
   (CIFAR-10 {{report.clamp.s2_cifar10_cnn_n_at_clamp}}, MNIST {{report.clamp.s2_mnist_cnn_n_at_clamp}}; the others have ratios down to
   {{checks.clamp_ratio.s2.cifar10.cfg1.ratio_min:.1f}}). But loss > ln 10 does not by itself mean an explosion: in CIFAR-10 mlp1 only
   {{checks.clamp_ratio.s2.cifar10.mlp1.n_at_clamp}} such runs are at the clamp, median ratio {{checks.clamp_ratio.s2.cifar10.mlp1.ratio_median:.2f}}.
2. **§1.5-2 ("abrupt, one epoch").** Only partly: ≤ 1 epoch from 10 % to 90 % dead in
   {{mechanism.q1.speed.all.share_le1:pct0}} % of collapses; {{report.speed.s2_mnist.share_le1:pct0}} % on MNIST CNN, {{report.speed.s2_h256.share_le1:pct0}} % at H256; Stage 0 H128
   median {{mechanism.q1.speed.s0_mnist_mlp2_h128.dur_median:.1f}} epochs, max {{mechanism.q1.speed.s0_mnist_mlp2_h128.dur_max:.0f}}. Fast, but not one epoch in general.
3. **§1.5-3 (leaky plateau).** Confirmed for mlp2 (output w_norm ×{{mechanism.q4.s0_h128_lr0.03.jump_output_med:.2g}} at H128 LR 0.03, logit
   spread {{report.q4.s0_mnist_mlp2_lr0.03.lbs_at:.2g}}, saturated softmax, ≤ {{mechanism.q4.s0_h128_lr0.03.leaky_dead_max:.3f}} dead). The jump factor depends strongly on
   LR (×{{mechanism.q4.s0_h128_lr0.02.jump_output_med:.2g}} to ×{{mechanism.q4.s0_h128_lr0.05.jump_output_med:.2g}}). The 85 % plateau is mlp2-specific; the CNN
   recovers on MNIST and ends near {{mechanism.q4.recovery.s2_cifar10_cfg1_lr0.02.val_final:.0f}} % on CIFAR-10.
4. **§1.5-4 (hybrid regimes).** Confirmed ({{mechanism.q2.s0_h128_lr0.03.hybrid_relu_dead_hidden2:.3f}} dead at LR 0.03,
   {{mechanism.q2.s0_h128_lr0.05.hybrid_relu_dead_hidden2:.2f}} at 0.05).
5. **§1.5-5 (mask placement).** Confirmed, with one addition: at CIFAR-10 H256 conv-only mixing
   is better than mixing both layers ({{tables.T3.cifar10.h256.stress.tanh+relu_minus_tanh+relu@conv.mean_diff:+.1f}} pp for both − @conv).
6. **§1.5-6 (mlp1).** Not as stated. CIFAR-10 mlp1 ReLU does not collapse late: its failures are
   never_learned ({{outcomes.depth.v6_stage2.cifar10.mlp1.relu.n_never_learned}}/{{outcomes.depth.v6_stage2.cifar10.mlp1.relu.n_never_learned:n}}; 0 late collapses). "Late collapse needs two ReLU
   layers" holds on both datasets.
7. **§1.5-7 (tuned accuracy).** MNIST {{report.t5.mnist.min:+.2f}} to {{report.t5.mnist.max:+.2f}} pp (one cell slightly beyond ±0.1);
   CIFAR-10 CNN H 64/128 {{report.t5.cifar10_cnn_h64_128.min:+.2f}} to {{report.t5.cifar10_cnn_h64_128.max:+.2f}} pp, so the lower end is below the stated 0.5 pp
   (cfg1 H128 {{tables.T5.cifar10.cfg1.h128.tanh+relu_minus_relu.mean_diff:+.2f}}, p_holm {{tables.T5.cifar10.cfg1.h128.tanh+relu_minus_relu.p_holm:p}}).
8. **A8 premises checked against the data.** "Hybrid beats tanh at H ≥ 128 at the stress LR"
   holds for CIFAR-10 cfg1 only (cfg2 H128 p_holm {{tables.T2.cifar10.cfg2.h128.stress.tanh.p_holm:p}}, H256
   {{tables.T2.cifar10.cfg2.h256.stress.tanh.p_holm:p}}). "ρ = 0.5 beats both parents at the stress LR" fails on MNIST (§5). "Sigmoid
   is the most stable in every setting" holds for failures ({{report.sigmoid_stress.n_fail}}) but not for the mean in
   one cell ({{report.sigmoid_stress.exceptions}}).
9. **New: ReLU failure is non-monotone in LR** in version5 and Stage 0 mlp2 H128
   ({{critical_lr.v6_stage0.mnist.mlp2.h128.relu.fail.display}}) and in the CIFAR-10 CNN
   ({{critical_lr.v6_stage2.cifar10.cfg2.h128.relu.fail.display}}): at higher LR some runs never learn and end just
   above 15 %.

## 8. Claims the data do not support

- A general tuned-accuracy gain from mixing (T5: none on MNIST, a cost on CIFAR-10 CNN at H 64/128).
- "Slow death" of ReLU units: the loss rises early, the units die fast.
- The training-loss rise as an early warning (false positives {{mechanism.q6.lossrise.all.fpr_same_cells:pct0}} % in the same cells;
  AUC {{mechanism.q6.s2.loss_rise.auc:.2f}} in Stage 2).
- "Very confident errors" behind leaky loss > ln 10: it is the 1e-12 clamp times the error rate.
- A single weight-norm threshold that predicts the leaky spike.
- Hybrid ≥ tanh everywhere: MNIST CNN H 64/128 tanh is higher at the stress LR.
- ρ = 0.5 as the best share in general.
- That bounding the conv output *causes* the CIFAR-10 protection (Q3 is correlational).
- A meaningful K choice in Stage 1 cfg1 (margins {{tables.T8.mnist.cfg1_margin_pp:.2f}} pp) or a robust CIFAR-10 cfg2 choice.
- Mixing as the most stable option at high LR: sigmoid is more stable in {{report.sigmoid_stress.n_top}}/{{report.sigmoid_stress.n_cells}} cells.

## 9. Open questions for Part B (new runs needed)

1. Causal test of the conv-layer effect: bound or normalise the conv output (clip, tanh-only conv,
   or per-channel normalisation) with an all-ReLU hidden layer, CIFAR-10 cfg2 H128, LR 0.01 / 0.02.
2. He initialisation (the current U(±1/√n) has 1/6 of the He variance).
3. Mini-batch SGD, momentum and Adam.
4. GELU, SiLU and ELU as single functions and as mixing partners.
5. A finer LR grid around the transitions (MNIST 0.01-0.05, CIFAR-10 0.005-0.03) to turn the
   brackets into fitted LR50s.
6. CIFAR-10 LR 1e-4 to un-censor the grid-edge selections.
7. Depth 3 (two hidden layers after the conv).
8. Fashion-MNIST as a third dataset.

